  "worked_solution": "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{\u2015}{x},\\overset{\u2015}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\u222c}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={\u222c}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={\u222c}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\u222c}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}y\\cdot \\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{{e}^{-y}}^{1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x]_{x={e}^{-y}}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}0-(-y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\u2015}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$",
